00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:39.260
Imagine you hold a Canadian bond ETF that tracks US treasuries and you see the 30-year yield drop 12 basis points the morning after Besson's announcement.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:50.440
Your order filled at the previous close yet the ETF's net asset value adjusted only partway because the underlying bonds traded in a thin after hours window with wide spreads.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:57.060
When the treasury buys $X billion in a specific maturity, it removes supply from that segment.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.820
Prices move up and yields move down in a mechanical way that does not require a fed rate cut.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:12.060
Most retail investors miss that the effect is localized to the purchased maturities and can reverse quickly once the program size is fully priced in.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:19.800
Professionals always check the treasury's announced purchase sizes and the exact maturities before assuming a broad rate rally.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.720
The misconception to avoid is treating every treasury announcement as a permanent policy shift.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:34.280
Size any rotation into rate sensitive holdings only after the first full session confirms the yield move with above average volume.

